From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:13:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: the dauth client prints the status and the start of the
 answer

The first call took 35 s against the mock and said nothing: the map matched, no connect error, no
handshake error, and an answer that was not empty. What is left to know is the status and the bytes,
so it prints both, once, for every call.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: the dauth client (0070) needs to say what a server answered, not only that it failed; this is the diagnostic that turns a silent failure into a status and a body
Issue: none opened yet — folds into the same PR as 0070

---
 dm/host/hle_dauth.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_dauth.c b/dm/host/hle_dauth.c
index 54825b4..64d48e9 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_dauth.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_dauth.c
@@ -129,7 +129,6 @@ static int http_call(const char *host, int port, const char *method, const char
     close(fd);
 
     int status = -1;
-    if (buf && !*buf) fprintf(stderr, "dauth: %s%s answered nothing\n", host, path);
     if (buf) {
         if (!strncmp(buf, "HTTP/1.", 7)) status = atoi(buf + 9);
         char *sep = strstr(buf, "\r\n\r\n");
@@ -142,7 +141,10 @@ static int http_call(const char *host, int port, const char *method, const char
                 out[len] = 0;
             }
         }
+        fprintf(stderr, "dauth: %s%s -> status %d, %zu bytes: %.60s\n", host, path, status, got, buf);
         free(buf);
+    } else {
+        fprintf(stderr, "dauth: %s%s -> no buffer\n", host, path);
     }
     return status;
 }
